//? Title: Lamport's Bakery Algorithm: Mutual Exclusion From Reads and Writes
//? Subtitle: Why taking a numbered ticket keeps threads apart, a model checker that confirms it and shows why the choosing flag is needed, and a real bakery lock, with datamunge's algorithms::bakery_model_check and algorithms::BakeryLock
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Lamport's bakery algorithm (1974) lets \(n\) threads share a critical section using nothing but
reads and writes of shared variables. A thread takes a ticket one larger than every ticket it
sees, then waits until its (ticket, id) pair is the smallest. We prove that this gives mutual
exclusion and first-come-first-served order, and point to the one step of the proof that needs
the `choosing` flag, which marks a thread that is still picking its ticket. An exhaustive search
of every interleaving of the protocol's atomic steps confirms the result for two and three
processes (38,172 states for three) and finds a 10-step schedule that puts two
processes in the critical section once the flag is removed. Random schedules show the tickets
and the service order, and a real lock built from C++ atomics protects a shared counter
correctly, at a few times the cost of `std::mutex`.
)

\toc

> The protocol

Each thread \(i\) owns two shared variables, written only by \(i\) and read by everyone:
\(\text{choosing}[i]\), initially false, and \(\text{number}[i]\), initially 0 (0 means "not
interested").

```
LOCK(i):
    choosing[i] ← true                                    # doorway
    number[i] ← 1 + max(number[0], …, number[n−1])          # reads one at a time
    choosing[i] ← false
    for each j ≠ i:                                       # bakery
        wait while choosing[j]
        wait while number[j] ≠ 0 and (number[j], j) < (number[i], i)
UNLOCK(i):
    number[i] ← 0
```

The pairs are compared lexicographically: a smaller ticket goes first, and equal tickets (two
threads can read the same maximum at the same time) are broken by id. The service order this
induces, for tickets already drawn, is what `bakery_service_order()` computes:

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  const std::vector<alg::BakeryTicket> tickets = {{3, 0}, {0, 1}, {2, 2}, {3, 3}, {2, 4}, {1, 5}};
  std::print("| thread | ticket |\n| ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& t : tickets) std::print("| {} | {} |\n", t.id, t.number ? std::to_string(t.number) : "– (idle)");
  std::print("\nService order:");
  for (auto id : alg::bakery_service_order(tickets)) std::print(" {}", id);
  std::print(". Keys distinct: {}.\n", alg::bakery_mutual_exclusion_holds(tickets));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| thread | ticket |
| ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 3 |
| 1 | – (idle) |
| 2 | 2 |
| 3 | 3 |
| 4 | 2 |
| 5 | 1 |

Service order: 5 2 4 0 3. Keys distinct: true.
// result_end

Thread 5 holds the only ticket 1 and goes first. Threads 2 and 4 both hold ticket 2, so the
lower id, 2, goes before 4; then threads 0 and 3 with ticket 3 in the same way. Thread 1 is idle
and is not served. The pairs are all distinct, which is what makes exactly one thread "first".

> Why it works

Say thread \(i\) has *finished the doorway* once it has set \(\text{choosing}[i]\) back to false
with its ticket written. The proof rests on one lemma.

**Lemma.** If thread \(i\) is in the critical section and thread \(k\neq i\) has finished its
doorway, then \((\text{number}[i],i)<(\text{number}[k],k)\).

*Proof sketch.* Consider the moment \(i\) last read \(\text{choosing}[k]=\text{false}\), during
its loop. Either \(k\) had not yet entered its doorway then, or \(k\) had already finished it.

- If \(k\) had not yet entered, then \(k\) entered later, after \(i\) had written its own ticket,
  so \(k\)'s scan of the tickets saw \(\text{number}[i]\) and \(k\) drew a strictly larger ticket.
- If \(k\) had finished its doorway, then \(\text{number}[k]\) was already final when \(i\) next
  read it. Since \(i\) got past its second wait, either \(\text{number}[k]=0\) (and \(k\) left and
  must draw again later, larger than \(i\)'s ticket as in the first case) or
  \((\text{number}[i],i)<(\text{number}[k],k)\). \(\square\)

**Mutual exclusion.** If \(i\) and \(k\) were both in the critical section, both have finished the
doorway, and the lemma gives both \((\text{number}[i],i)<(\text{number}[k],k)\) and the reverse,
which is impossible because the pairs are distinct.

**First come, first served.** If \(i\) finishes its doorway before \(k\) starts its doorway, \(k\)
reads \(i\)'s ticket and draws a larger one, so \(k\) waits for \(i\). Nobody starves: a waiting
thread's ticket is fixed, and every thread that arrives later draws a larger one.

The `choosing` flag is used in exactly one place: the first case split. Without it, \(i\) cannot
tell a thread that has not started its doorway from one that has read the tickets but not yet
written its own. Such a thread may be about to write a ticket *equal* to \(i\)'s with a smaller id,
and then both believe they go first.

> Checking every interleaving

The proof treats each read and write as atomic, as the hardware guarantees for single words with
sequentially consistent ordering. `bakery_model_check()` explores every interleaving of such
steps by breadth-first search over the states of all processes: program counters, loop indices,
partial maxima, flags and tickets. Tickets grow without bound when processes keep returning, so
draws above a bound are cut off; every interleaving that stays within the bound is explored.
The block checks two and three processes with and without the flag, and prints the shortest
violating schedule found.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <chrono>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::print("| processes | choosing flag | ticket bound | states | transitions | draws cut | most in critical section | mutual exclusion | s |\n"
             "| ---: | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: |\n");
  alg::BakeryCheck shown;
  for (auto [n, flag, bound] : {std::tuple{2, true, 8}, {2, false, 8}, {3, true, 5}, {3, false, 5}}) {
    const auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    auto r = alg::bakery_model_check({static_cast<std::size_t>(n), flag, static_cast<std::uint32_t>(bound)});
    const double s = std::chrono::duration<double>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - t0).count();
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {:.1f} |\n", n, flag ? "yes" : "no", bound, r.states, r.transitions,
               r.cut_by_bound, r.max_critical, r.mutual_exclusion ? "holds" : "**violated**", s);
    if (n == 2 && !flag) shown = std::move(r);
  }
  std::print("\nShortest violating schedule with 2 processes and no choosing flag:\n\n| step | process | action |\n| ---: | ---: | --- |\n");
  for (std::size_t k = 0; k < shown.counterexample.size(); ++k)
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", k + 1, shown.counterexample[k].process, shown.counterexample[k].action);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| processes | choosing flag | ticket bound | states | transitions | draws cut | most in critical section | mutual exclusion | s |
| ---: | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: |
| 2 | yes | 8 | 906 | 1584 | 20 | 1 | holds | 0.0 |
| 2 | no | 8 | 568 | 1082 | 18 | 2 | **violated** | 0.0 |
| 3 | yes | 5 | 38172 | 97861 | 1615 | 1 | holds | 0.0 |
| 3 | no | 5 | 36403 | 102714 | 2574 | 3 | **violated** | 0.0 |

Shortest violating schedule with 2 processes and no choosing flag:

| step | process | action |
| ---: | ---: | --- |
| 1 | 0 | start doorway |
| 2 | 0 | read number[0] = 0 |
| 3 | 0 | read number[1] = 0 |
| 4 | 1 | start doorway |
| 5 | 1 | read number[0] = 0 |
| 6 | 1 | read number[1] = 0 |
| 7 | 1 | number[1] = 1 |
| 8 | 1 | read number[0] = 0, pass, enter critical section |
| 9 | 0 | number[0] = 1 |
| 10 | 0 | read number[1] = 1, pass, enter critical section |
// result_end

With the flag, every reachable state has at most one process in the critical section: 906
states for two processes and 38,172 for three, explored in well under a second. Without it, the
search finds a violation for two processes and, for three, states with all three inside at once.

The shortest violating schedule is exactly the gap in the proof. Process 0 reads both tickets
(both 0) but has not yet written its own. Process 1 then runs its whole doorway, takes ticket 1,
sees \(\text{number}[0]=0\) and enters. Process 0 now writes ticket 1, *equal* to process 1's, and
since its id is smaller, \((1,0)<(1,1)\) and it enters too. With the flag, process 1 would have
seen \(\text{choosing}[0]=\text{true}\) at step 8 and waited until process 0's ticket was written,
then deferred to it.

The ticket bound cuts off only draws above it: 20 of 1,584 transitions for two processes. The
check is therefore complete for schedules whose tickets stay small, which is where the
interesting races are, since the violations above need only tickets of 1.

> Watching a schedule

`bakery_simulate()` runs the same atomic steps under a random scheduler that picks a process
uniformly at each step. The block runs four processes for 100,000 steps and draws the first
400.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bakery_schedule.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::size_t n = 4, steps = 100000, shown = 400;
  const auto sim = alg::bakery_simulate({n, true, 0}, steps, 2026);
  std::uint32_t max_ticket = 0;
  for (const auto& row : sim.number)
    for (auto v : row) max_ticket = std::max(max_ticket, v);
  std::print("| process | critical-section entries |\n| ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) std::print("| {} | {} |\n", i, sim.entries[i]);
  std::print("\nSteps with two processes in the critical section: {}. Largest ticket: {}.\n", sim.violations, max_ticket);
  // Longest run of consecutive entries by the same process.
  std::size_t run = 1, longest = 1;
  for (std::size_t k = 1; k < sim.order.size(); ++k) {
    run = sim.order[k] == sim.order[k - 1] ? run + 1 : 1;
    longest = std::max(longest, run);
  }
  std::print("Longest run of consecutive entries by one process: {}.\n", longest);

  std::vector<double> xs, ys;
  for (std::size_t t = 0; t < shown; ++t) xs.push_back(static_cast<double>(t));
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) ys.push_back(static_cast<double>(i));
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> z(shown, std::vector<double>(n));
  for (std::size_t t = 0; t < shown; ++t)
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) z[t][i] = static_cast<double>(sim.phase[t][i]);
  auto phases = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                    .image(xs, ys, z, {dm::RGB{241, 245, 249}, dm::RGB{253, 186, 116}, dm::RGB{147, 197, 253}, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}}, 0, 3)
                    .legend({"idle", "doorway", "waiting", "critical"},
                            {dm::RGB{241, 245, 249}, dm::RGB{253, 186, 116}, dm::RGB{147, 197, 253}, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}})
                    .y_ticks({0, 1, 2, 3}, {"0", "1", "2", "3"})
                    .y_limits(-0.5, 5.2)
                    .title("Phase of each process")
                    .x_label("step")
                    .y_label("process");
  auto tickets = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  const dm::RGB colours[] = {{37, 99, 235}, {22, 163, 74}, {202, 138, 4}, {147, 51, 234}};
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
    std::vector<double> v;
    for (std::size_t t = 0; t < shown; ++t) v.push_back(sim.number[t][i]);
    tickets.lines(xs, v, "", colours[i], 2.0);
  }
  tickets.legend({"process 0", "process 1", "process 2", "process 3"}, {colours[0], colours[1], colours[2], colours[3]})
      .y_limits(0, 21)
      .title("Tickets (0 = not requesting)")
      .x_label("step")
      .y_label("number[i]");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(2, 1).add(phases).add(tickets).size(1000, 760).save_svg("build/bakery_schedule.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| process | critical-section entries |
| ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 1060 |
| 1 | 1027 |
| 2 | 997 |
| 3 | 993 |

Steps with two processes in the critical section: 0. Largest ticket: 1343.
Longest run of consecutive entries by one process: 2.
\image(figures/bakery_schedule.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The first 400 steps of a random schedule of four processes. Top: each process's phase (idle, doorway, waiting, critical). Bottom: each process's ticket.)
\alttext(Two panels over steps 0 to 400. Top: four horizontal bands, one per process, coloured by phase: mostly long blue waiting stretches, orange doorway stretches and thin red critical stretches, with the red stretches never overlapping between processes. Bottom: the four processes' tickets as step lines, each ticket held for a while then dropping to 0, with successive tickets climbing from 1 to about 15 over the 400 steps.)

In 100,000 random steps no two processes were ever in the critical section together, and the
four processes entered it 993 to 1,060 times each: service is fair. No process entered more than
twice in a row, because each departing process must draw a new ticket larger than those already
waiting. In the figure the red critical stretches are thin and never overlap, and most of each
process's time is spent waiting, since with four processes competing, three are usually queued.

The bottom panel shows the algorithm's best-known weakness: the tickets keep climbing, reaching
1,343 by the end. A ticket only drops back when the bakery empties completely, which under
constant contention never happens, so the variables must be wide enough for unbounded growth.
Bounded variants exist, at the cost of more complicated protocols.

> A real lock

`BakeryLock` implements the protocol with `std::atomic` loads and stores, which are sequentially
consistent by default; with weaker memory orderings the processor could reorder a thread's ticket
write after its reads of other tickets, and the proof would no longer apply. The block has each of
\(T\) threads increment a plain shared `long` 200,000 times inside the lock, and compares with
`std::mutex` and with no lock at all.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <chrono>
#include <mutex>
#include <thread>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

template <typename Lock, typename Unlock>
std::pair<long, double> run(std::size_t threads, std::size_t per, Lock lock, Unlock unlock) {
  long counter = 0;
  const auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
  std::vector<std::thread> pool;
  for (std::size_t t = 0; t < threads; ++t)
    pool.emplace_back([&, t] {
      for (std::size_t k = 0; k < per; ++k) {
        lock(t);
        volatile long v = counter;
        counter = v + 1;
        unlock(t);
      }
    });
  for (auto& th : pool) th.join();
  const double ns = std::chrono::duration<double, std::nano>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - t0).count() / (threads * per);
  return {counter, ns};
}

int main() {
  const std::size_t per = 200000;
  std::print("Hardware threads: {}.\n\n", std::thread::hardware_concurrency());
  std::print("| threads | expected count | bakery count | bakery ns per increment | largest ticket | std::mutex count | mutex ns | no lock count |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t T : {1u, 2u, 4u}) {
    alg::BakeryLock bakery(T);
    std::mutex m;
    const auto b = run(T, per, [&](std::size_t i) { bakery.lock(i); }, [&](std::size_t i) { bakery.unlock(i); });
    const auto x = run(T, per, [&](std::size_t) { m.lock(); }, [&](std::size_t) { m.unlock(); });
    const auto u = run(T, per, [](std::size_t) {}, [](std::size_t) {});
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {:.0f} | {} | {} | {:.0f} | {} |\n", T, T * per, b.first, b.second, bakery.max_ticket(), x.first,
               x.second, u.first);
  }
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Hardware threads: 32.

| threads | expected count | bakery count | bakery ns per increment | largest ticket | std::mutex count | mutex ns | no lock count |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 200000 | 200000 | 21 | 1 | 200000 | 13 | 200000 |
| 2 | 400000 | 400000 | 352 | 107 | 400000 | 50 | 268897 |
| 4 | 800000 | 800000 | 410 | 106303 | 800000 | 71 | 234315 |
// result_end

With the bakery lock every increment survived: the counter equals \(T\times200{,}000\) for one,
two and four threads, while without a lock four threads lost between half and three quarters of their increments. The cost is
higher than `std::mutex`: on this machine a locked increment takes roughly 20 ns against 10 for
one thread and roughly 200 ns against 50 for four, and the ratio varies between about 2 and 8
from run to run as the threads contend. Each acquisition reads every other thread's ticket and
flag, so the work grows with \(T\), and the cache lines holding those variables bounce between
cores; a mutex built on an atomic read-modify-write needs one contended line. The largest ticket
with four threads runs to tens or hundreds of thousands after 800,000 acquisitions, again showing the unbounded growth.
The bakery algorithm's value is that it needs no such instruction: it shows that reads and
writes alone are enough for fair mutual exclusion.

> Summary

- A thread takes a ticket one larger than all it sees and waits for the smallest
  (ticket, id) pair; the lemma that the critical thread's pair is smaller than every other
  finished doorway gives mutual exclusion and first-come-first-served order.
- An exhaustive search of interleavings confirms mutual exclusion for two and three processes
  (906 and 38,172 states). Without the `choosing` flag it finds a 10-step schedule in which a
  thread that has read the tickets but not written its own enters alongside another.
- Random schedules serve four processes evenly with no violations, but tickets grow without
  bound under contention.
- A `std::atomic` implementation is correct and costs a few times as much as `std::mutex` on
  one to four threads, growing with the number of threads.
